<DocsPage
  title="Primitives"
  description="Gimble supplies runtime primitives, not named tactics. Eight of them cover most multi-agent work."
  eyebrow="Feature"
  {headings}
>

Signatures and edge cases live in Godoc: `go doc -all github.com/tylergannon/gimble`. This page says what each primitive is for.

<h2 id="scope">Scopes</h2>

`Scope(ctx, name, body)` runs `body` in a named child scope. When `body` returns, the scope's services stop, its sessions close through their adapters, and its context is cancelled. A scope is the unit of ownership, of context, and of the map: every sheet in the console is one.

```go
err := gimble.Scope(ctx, "research", func(ctx context.Context) error {
	researcher := gimble.NewSession(ctx, roleResearch, env.WorkDir)
	// researcher is closed when this function returns
	return nil
})
```

<h2 id="context">Scoped context delivery</h2>

`Set` and `SetJSON` record a value in the current scope, once. `Generate` appends the scope's rendered context to the prompt, so every agent turn in that scope, and in its children, receives the facts without the workflow threading them through. Prompts stay constants; data stays data.

```go
gimble.Set(ctx, "promise", promise)
gimble.Set(ctx, "repository", env.WorkDir)
// every turn below this line is sent both values
```

A key is set once per scope; revision is shadowing in a child scope. `WithScopeTemplate` reshapes the rendered context for one call, with a template that is itself a constant.

<figure>
  <Shot src="shots/context.png" alt="A task scope selected in the console, showing its assignment and the values written in it." label="a task scope, selected" />
  <figcaption>A task scope in the console: its assignment and the values written in it, including captured command output.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="promise-loop">Promise loop</h2>

`PromiseLoop(ctx, name, goal, planner)` opens planner-directed dispatch toward a goal. The planner keeps a revisable backlog and selects each task from the evidence so far. You range over `loop.Tasks`; each task arrives with its own scope, a description, a definition of done, and optional validation. Check `loop.Err()` afterward.

```go
loop := gimble.PromiseLoop(ctx, "implementation", promise, planner)
for taskCtx, task := range loop.Tasks {
	// do the task, record what happened in taskCtx, break when done
}
if err := loop.Err(); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

What the worker and validator record in the task's scope is what the planner sees when it picks the next task. An operator can message the loop while it runs; the message waits for the planner's next decision.

<h2 id="supervision">Supervision</h2>

`WithSupervisor(session, instruction)` attaches a second agent to a running turn. While the worker works, the supervisor looks at what it did since the last look, and each objection is steered into the live turn. When the worker finishes, the supervisors are cancelled and joined before `Generate` returns.

```go
report, err := worker.Generate[gimble.Text](taskCtx, implementationPrompt,
	gimble.WithSupervisor(coach, scopePrompt, gimble.WithInterval(2*time.Minute)))
```

Supervision is advisory. It catches scope drift while it is cheap to correct, and it never gates completion. It attaches to a planner's turns too, and a supervisor can have supervisors.

<figure>
  <Shot src="shots/supervision.png" alt="A supervisor selected in the console." label="a watcher, selected" />
</figure>

<h2 id="graph">Statically mapped graph</h2>

`go generate` reads the workflow function's source into a typed `workflow.Graph` literal: sessions, agent calls with their prompts, loops, groups, conditions, commands, and supervisors, each with its file and line. The literal registers itself by name.

At run time, events are joined to that graph by name. The console therefore shows the whole workflow before the first turn, marks what has not started, and links every node to the line that made it. There is no graph file to write and none to drift.

<h2 id="concurrency">Observable concurrency</h2>

`Group` is errgroup-style concurrency whose branches are scopes: each `Go` gets a name, a lifetime, and a place on the map, and `Wait` joins them. `Iterate` yields each item of a finite slice with a fresh scope, in order, closing that item's sessions before the next.

```go
group := gimble.Group(ctx, "drafts")
group.Go("draft", func(ctx context.Context) error { /* ... */ return nil })
group.Go("draft", func(ctx context.Context) error { /* ... */ return nil })
return group.Wait()
```

<h2 id="evidence">Evidence and services</h2>

`Check` runs a command and records its exit code, stdout, and stderr in the current scope for the next agent turn to assess. A nonzero exit is evidence, not an error. `RunCommand` returns the same values to Go instead.

`Service` starts a foreground command, such as a dev server, and makes the current scope own it. It stops when the scope ends. If it exits early, the scope fails.

```go
gimble.Service(ctx, "dev-server", env.WorkDir, "pnpm dev")
gimble.Check(ctx, "tests", env.WorkDir, "go", "test", "./...")
```

<h2 id="interview">Interviews</h2>

`Interview(ctx, name, session, purpose)` lets an agent question a person. Each question is a typed session turn; the run blocks until the console delivers an answer, and an empty answer ends the interview. The transcript comes back as a value.

</DocsPage>
